    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$a^m\\times a^n=a^{m+n}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$, $n=\\frac{r}{s}$ and $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}$. Integer-power laws give $a^ma^n=u^{ps+rq}=a^{m+n}$.",
          "worked": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$ and $n=\\frac{r}{s}$, where $p,r\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $q,s\\in\\mathbb N$. First take $a>0$. Put $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}$. The root definition gives $a^m=u^{ps}$ and $a^n=u^{rq}$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}a^ma^n&=u^{ps}u^{rq}\\\\a^ma^n&=u^{ps+rq}\\\\a^ma^n&=a^{(ps+rq)/(qs)}\\\\a^ma^n&=a^{m+n}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a=0$, the law also holds whenever both original powers are defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}$, if $a\\ne0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "With $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}$, integer-power division gives $\\frac{a^m}{a^n}=u^{ps-rq}=a^{m-n}$.",
          "worked": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$ and $n=\\frac{r}{s}$, with $q,s\\in\\mathbb N$. Since $a>0$, put $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}>0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a^m}{a^n}&=\\frac{u^{ps}}{u^{rq}}\\\\\\frac{a^m}{a^n}&=u^{ps-rq}\\\\\\frac{a^m}{a^n}&=a^{(ps-rq)/(qs)}\\\\\\frac{a^m}{a^n}&=a^{m-n}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(a^m)^n=a^{mn}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Put $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}$. Since $(u^{pr})^s=(a^m)^r$, the root definition gives $(a^m)^n=u^{pr}=a^{mn}$.",
          "worked": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$ and $n=\\frac{r}{s}$, with $q,s\\in\\mathbb N$. For $a>0$, put $u=\\sqrt[qs]{a}>0$. Then $a^m=u^{ps}$, and integer-power laws give\n$$\\begin{align*}(u^{pr})^s&=u^{prs}\\\\(u^{pr})^s&=(u^{ps})^r\\\\(u^{pr})^s&=(a^m)^r.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $u^{pr}$ is the positive $s$th root of $(a^m)^r$. By the rational-power definition,\n$$\\begin{align*}(a^m)^n&=u^{pr}\\\\(a^m)^n&=a^{pr/(qs)}\\\\(a^m)^n&=a^{mn}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe case $a=0$ also holds whenever the original expressions are defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(ab)^m=a^mb^m$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The non-negative $q$th root of $ab$ is $\\sqrt[q]{a}\\sqrt[q]{b}$. Raise this equality to the integer power $p$.",
          "worked": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$, with $q\\in\\mathbb N$. Put $u=\\sqrt[q]{a}$ and $v=\\sqrt[q]{b}$. Since\n$$\\begin{align*}(uv)^q&=u^qv^q\\\\(uv)^q&=ab,\\end{align*}$$\nand $uv\\geq0$, uniqueness of the non-negative root gives $\\sqrt[q]{ab}=uv$. Therefore, wherever the powers are defined,\n$$\\begin{align*}(ab)^m&=(uv)^p\\\\(ab)^m&=u^pv^p\\\\(ab)^m&=a^mb^m.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q18-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\left(\\dfrac ab\\right)^m=\\dfrac{a^m}{b^m}$, if $b\\ne0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The non-negative $q$th root of $\\frac{a}{b}$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt[q]{a}}{\\sqrt[q]{b}}$. Raise this equality to the integer power $p$.",
          "worked": "Write $m=\\frac{p}{q}$, with $q\\in\\mathbb N$. Put $u=\\sqrt[q]{a}$ and $v=\\sqrt[q]{b}>0$. Since\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac uv\\right)^q&=\\frac{u^q}{v^q}\\\\\\left(\\frac uv\\right)^q&=\\frac ab,\\end{align*}$$\nand $\\frac{u}{v}\\geq0$, uniqueness of the non-negative root gives $\\sqrt[q]{\\frac{a}{b}}=\\frac{u}{v}$. Therefore, wherever the powers are defined,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac ab\\right)^m&=\\left(\\frac uv\\right)^p\\\\\\left(\\frac ab\\right)^m&=\\frac{u^p}{v^p}\\\\\\left(\\frac ab\\right)^m&=\\frac{a^m}{b^m}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
  },
